.\"{{{}}}
.\"{{{  Title
.TH MGRLOAD 1 "October 24, 1992" "" "MGR Programmer's Manual"
.\"}}}
.\"{{{  Name
.SH NAME
mgrload -\ Graph of system load average
.\"}}}
.\"{{{  Synopsis
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mgrload
.\"}}}
.\"{{{  Description
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Mgrload
displays the current CPU load: the average number of processes wanting the
processor over the past 60 seconds, decayed towards each new sample.
.B Mgrload
represents this data as a scrolling bar graph, one bar per sample.  The
vertical scale of the graph is dynamically adjusted to account for changing
load average values.
.PP
On COHERENT the load is sampled every 5 seconds from the kernel's own process
chain, read through
.IR nlist (3)
on the kernel image
.B /coherent
and the process chain in
.BR /dev/kmem ,
exactly as
.IR ps (1)
reads them.
.B /coherent
must be the kernel that is running.  If it is not, or if the chain read out of
.B /dev/kmem
does not lie in the kernel's allocation arena,
.B mgrload
draws a flat zero rather than a fabricated load.
.PP
.B /dev/kmem
is mode 600, owned by root, so
.B mgrload
is installed set-uid root.  It opens the file as its first action, before it
speaks to the window server at all, and gives the privilege up immediately
afterwards; everything it does thereafter it does as the user who ran it.
.PP
The load is carried in hundredths of a process.  One graph partition is a
whole process, so the graph cannot resolve a hundredth; no floating point
arithmetic is involved.
.\"}}}
